<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Three spells of loss of awareness in eighteen months in a 54-year-old man, the latest with stiffening and jerking of both arms, a bitten tongue, and twenty minutes of confusion. Please advise whether these are seizures, convulsive syncope, or transient ischemic attacks, interpret the EEG and MRI, and advise on treatment, stroke prevention, and driving. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent, within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Are these spells seizures, convulsive syncope, or transient ischemic attacks?</item><item>If seizures, how should they be classified, and is this epilepsy?</item><item>What do the EEG, MRI, and vascular imaging show, and what is the cause?</item><item>Should he start an antiseizure medicine now, and which one?</item><item>Does he need stroke prevention, and what should it include?</item><item>What should he be told about driving, riding, swimming, and safety at work?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Julius Caesar is seen at the request of Dr. Fairbanks-Okonkwo, two weeks after a witnessed collapse. I took a fuller history from him and from the member of his staff who saw the latest spell. All three spells began with the same rising feeling in the upper abdomen, sometimes with a sense that things were strange or familiar; in two he stared and smacked his lips for a minute or so without responding; in the latest the arms then stiffened and jerked for about a minute. Each was followed by confusion of many minutes, and the latest by a bitten tongue and sore shoulders. None began with pallor, sweating, or greying vision, and none followed pain or a sudden change of posture; he had been standing for a long time in heat at the last. He has never had a sudden weakness, numbness, loss of speech, or loss of vision lasting minutes. He wakes every few weeks from vivid, frightening dreams, confused for a minute or two. He has occasional mild headaches. No head injury, meningitis, or febrile seizures in childhood that he knows of. He has had no spell since the first visit. He does not drive at present. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Gaius Julius Caesar was born on 12 or 13 July 100 BC in the Subura district of Rome, into the patrician family of the Julii. [D]</item><item>Suetonius writes that he lost his father &apos;in the course of his sixteenth year&apos;. [D]</item><item>Pliny the Elder records that two men of the family, one of them his father, a former praetor, &apos;died without any apparent cause, in the morning, while putting on their shoes&apos;. [D]</item><item>As a young man, in hiding from Sulla&apos;s agents, he was &apos;suffering from a severe attack of quartan ague&apos; and changed his hiding place almost every night. [D]</item><item>Plutarch describes him as &apos;of a spare habit&apos;, with &apos;a soft and white skin&apos;, and says he &apos;suffered from distemper in the head, and was subject to epileptic fits&apos;, the first of which attacked him at Corduba; he made &apos;his military service a cure for his feeble health&apos;. [D]</item><item>Suetonius says he was tall, of sound health, &apos;except that towards the end he was subject to sudden fainting fits and to nightmare as well&apos;, and that he was &apos;twice attacked by the falling sickness&apos; (comitialis morbus) during his campaigns. [D]</item><item>Plutarch gives a second account of the battle of Thapsus in Africa in 46 BC, in which &apos;his usual sickness laid hold of him&apos; as he was drawing up his army, and, sensing it coming, he was carried to a nearby tower and stayed there during the battle. [D]</item><item>He drank very little wine: &apos;not even his enemies denied&apos; it. [D]</item><item>His only daughter, Julia, died in childbirth in 54 BC. [D]</item><item>After the African campaign he returned to Rome in July 46 BC, celebrated four triumphs within a single month, and left Rome for Further Spain early in November 46 BC. [D]</item><item>He reformed the Roman calendar, which had drifted so far that the harvest festivals no longer came in summer, to a year of 365 days with a day added every fourth year; to realign it he lengthened 46 BC to 445 days, and the new calendar took effect on 1 January 45 BC. [D]</item><item>Retrospective readings take the attacks as epilepsy: one review counts four attacks that were probably complex partial (focal impaired-awareness) seizures and suggests an inherited cause; another treats them as late-onset epilepsy beginning at about 54 and proposes neurocysticercosis. [R]</item><item>Galassi and Ashrafian (2015) argued that his symptoms (headache, vertigo, falls, limb weakness, the collapse at Thapsus, and the giddiness at the Senate) fit cerebrovascular disease with transient ischemic attacks better than epilepsy, and that the sudden deaths in his family suggest cardiovascular disease. [R]</item><item>Other proposed causes include a brain arteriovenous malformation, a brain tumor such as a meningioma, celiac disease, and malaria. [R]</item></list><paragraph>2 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

