<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Focal epilepsy with a structural vascular cause (G40.209). These are seizures. The stereotyped rising epigastric aura, staring with oral automatisms, and prolonged confusion are typical of focal seizures from the temporal lobe, and the latest evolved to a bilateral tonic-clonic seizure. Convulsive syncope is brief, follows a prodrome of pallor and greying vision, and recovers within a minute or two; his rhythm, echocardiogram, and orthostatic readings are normal. Transient ischemic attacks cause loss of function, not loss of awareness with automatisms. ILAE 2017: focal impaired-awareness seizures with automatisms and a focal to bilateral tonic-clonic seizure; structural (vascular) cause. Three unprovoked seizures meet the ILAE definition of epilepsy; the brain lesion and the epileptiform EEG both raise the risk of recurrence. The vivid dreams with confused awakening may be nocturnal seizures and should go in the seizure diary. [S]</item><item>Chronic left posterior temporal cortical infarct (I69.398). The left temporal infarct matches the EEG and is the likely seizure focus. It was never recognized as a stroke, but because it now causes epilepsy I would not treat it as a silent infarct; I advise secondary stroke prevention as after an ischemic stroke of undetermined cause. No source of embolism so far; prolonged monitoring is still needed for atrial fibrillation. Moderate white matter disease fits long-standing untreated hypertension. [S]</item><item>Hypertension (I10). The main modifiable cause of his small-vessel disease; the target is below 130/80 mmHg. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: The emergency department notes (normal CT of the head, glucose, and sodium); the intake ECG; the 14-day ambulatory ECG (sinus rhythm throughout, a marked event of light-headedness on standing in sinus rhythm); the EEG with sleep (intermittent left temporal sharp waves in drowsiness and light sleep, normal background); MRI of the brain (no acute infarct; a chronic 1.5 cm cortical and subcortical infarct with gliosis in the left posterior temporal lobe; moderate white matter hyperintensities; no microbleeds, mass, vascular malformation, cyst, or calcification; normal hippocampi); CT angiography of the head and neck (calcified left carotid bifurcation plaque under 30 percent; no other stenosis, dissection, aneurysm, or malformation); the echocardiogram (ejection fraction 60 percent, mild concentric hypertrophy, normal left atrium, no thrombus or shunt); intake laboratory tests; and today&apos;s potassium 4.5 mmol/L and creatinine 1.02 mg/dL. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Start levetiracetam 500 mg twice daily today: effective for focal seizures, no titration needed, and no interactions with his other medicines. Warn about irritability and low mood, check mood at four weeks, and keep a seizure diary. Lamotrigine is the alternative if mood side effects occur. [S]</item><item>Aspirin 81 mg daily and atorvastatin 80 mg at bedtime, with a lipid panel at 4 to 12 weeks and an LDL target below 70 mg/dL; dual antiplatelet therapy is not indicated for an old infarct. [S]</item><item>Blood pressure below 130/80 mmHg: continue lisinopril and add a second medicine, such as amlodipine, if he stays above target. [S]</item><item>A second ambulatory monitor for at least four more weeks, for more than 30 days in all, to exclude atrial fibrillation before settling on aspirin alone. [S]</item><item>Screen for sleep apnea (STOP-Bang, with a home sleep test if intermediate or high), check mood with the PHQ-9, and screen cognition with the MoCA in the first months. [S]</item><item>No driving until he has been seizure-free for the period state law requires (six months in this state) and I have cleared him; no swimming or riding alone, no climbing to heights, showers rather than baths, regular sleep, and a written seizure action plan for his staff, with emergency services called for a seizure lasting more than five minutes or a second seizure. [S]</item><item>Neurology review at six months, sooner for any seizure. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Fairbanks-Okonkwo the same day. Julius Caesar understands the diagnosis, agrees to start levetiracetam, aspirin, and atorvastatin today, and accepts the driving restriction, though it frustrates him. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Primary care continues with Dr. Fairbanks-Okonkwo. Neurology review at six months, sooner for any seizure, a confused awakening that is clearly a seizure, or side effects. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
